  <h2 id="land">First: what kind of land?</h2>
  <p>In Andalusia, the town's zoning plan (<em>PGOU</em>) decides what may be built on a plot. The key question is what kind of land it is:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Suelo urbano</strong>: land in a village, town or urbanización. You may build here, within the zoning rules: how many square metres, how high and how far from the boundary.</li>
    <li><strong>Suelo rústico</strong>: rural land. Most fincas in the Axarquía stand on it. Building is the exception: the land is meant for farming and nature, not houses.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>You can find the type of land in the land registry (<em>Catastro</em>). An <em>informe urbanístico</em> from the town hall gives more certainty: it also shows how much has already been built on your plot and how much more is allowed.</p>
  <p>If you live in an apartment or an urbanización with an owners' community (<em>comunidad de propietarios</em>), the community's rules apply as well.</p>

