  <h2 id="urbano">In a residential area (urbano)</h2>
  <p>In a village or urbanización you may build, but the zoning plan decides how much. Three terms keep coming back:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Edificabilidad</strong>: how many square metres may be built on the plot in total. Once that is used up, nothing more may be added, not even a garage.</li>
    <li><strong>Ocupación</strong>: how much of the plot may be covered by buildings.</li>
    <li><strong>Retranqueos</strong>: the distance from the boundary and the street. Often a few metres, and a carport or garage must respect it too.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A canopy that is open at the sides only partly counts in many zoning plans. An enclosed garage or room counts in full. Rules differ per town, so have it checked first. Almost anything with a solid roof or walls needs a permit (<em>licencia de obra</em>), and larger works also need an architect. See our guide to <MaybeLink href="/en/guides/building-permits-renovation-andalusia/">building permits for renovation</MaybeLink>.</p>

  <h2 id="rustico">On a finca (rústico)</h2>
  <p>On rural land the rule is: you may only build what the law and the zoning plan expressly allow. For most fincas that means:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>A legal house</strong> may be maintained and renovated. Extending is only possible if the zoning plan leaves room, and many older fincas are already at the limit.</li>
    <li><strong>A new, separate building</strong> such as a garage or guest house needs a permit, and often first a separate authorisation for building on rural land. That takes a long time and often fails.</li>
    <li><strong>A second home</strong> on the same plot is almost never possible in practice. Under the Andalusian law LISTA, a new house on rústico requires among other things a large plot (the town sets the minimum, often several hectares), a separate procedure and a levy paid to the town.</li>
    <li><strong>A farm building</strong> (<em>almacén agrícola</em> or <em>cuarto de aperos</em>) is sometimes allowed, but only for farming. Turning it into a bedroom or guest house later is illegal.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>If your house has AFO status, you may not add anything. Read more in our guide to <a href="/en/guides/afo-legalisation-andalusia/">AFO and legalisation</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="community">In a complex: the community</h2>
  <p>If you live in an apartment complex or an urbanización with a <em>comunidad de propietarios</em>, the community's rules apply on top of the town's:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Statutes and house rules</strong> (<em>estatutos</em> and <em>normas de régimen interior</em>) can prohibit all sorts of things: a barbecue on the terrace, an awning in a different colour, a pergola on the roof terrace.</li>
    <li><strong>Anything that changes the façade or the building</strong>, such as enclosing a terrace or adding a floor, needs the owners' meeting to agree under the Spanish Horizontal Property Act (<em>Ley de Propiedad Horizontal</em>), usually by three fifths of the owners.</li>
    <li><strong>Nuisance</strong>: even if something is not expressly prohibited, the community can act against activities that bother the neighbours, such as smoke.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Ask the property manager (<em>administrador de fincas</em>) for the statutes, the house rules and the latest minutes before you buy or build. More on barbecues in <a href="/en/guides/building-on-your-own-land-spain/barbecue-in-apartments/">barbecues in apartment complexes</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="without">Building without a permit</h2>
  <p>There are thousands of carports, casitas and enclosed terraces in the Axarquía without a permit. That does not make it risk-free:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The town can fine you and order demolition for up to six years after completion. On protected land that period never runs out.</li>
    <li>When you sell, what stands on the plot must match the land registry and the deeds. An illegal extension then costs you time, money or buyers.</li>
    <li>An insurer may refuse to pay for damage to an unpermitted part.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>If something has already been built without a permit, legalising it is sometimes possible. See <a href="/en/guides/afo-legalisation-andalusia/">legalisation and AFO</a>. Buying a house? Have a <a href="/en/property-survey/">property survey</a> check that everything on the plot is also on paper.</p>

